---
/**
 * /about/methodology — how research on this site is actually produced.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/url';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const sources = await getCollection('sources');
const tierCount = (tier: string) => sources.filter((s) => s.data.tier === tier).length;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How Arthanomy researches, dates, classifies and verifies every claim — the source-tier system, the four-lane return framework, the calculator test suite and the review cycle."
  path="/about/methodology"
>
  <header class="shell pt-10">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 max-w-3xl font-serif text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How the research is made</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-[var(--color-ink-muted)]">
      Not everything can be verified equally. The method here is to say, for every claim, what kind of evidence
      supports it, when that evidence was published, and when it was last checked.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="shell article-body mt-10 max-w-3xl pb-16">
    <h2>1 · Sources, tiered</h2>
    <p>
      All {sources.length} sources live in a public <a href={href('/sources')}>registry</a>, classified by what they can
      establish: <strong>primary-official</strong> ({tierCount('primary-official')}) for statutes, regulators and
      government statistics; <strong>audited-disclosure</strong> ({tierCount('audited-disclosure')}) for filed
      financials and scheme documents; <strong>academic</strong> ({tierCount('academic')}) for peer-reviewed theory;
      <strong> reputable-secondary</strong> ({tierCount('reputable-secondary')}) for context; and further tiers down to
      <strong>market-commentary</strong> and <strong>unverified</strong> — the last used only to mark a gap, never to
      certify a number.
    </p>

    <h2>2 · Dates are part of every fact</h2>
    <p>
      Indian tax and regulatory facts expire. Each rule records when it took effect, which Act governs which period,
      and when it was last verified. A fact without a date is treated as a claim, not a fact.
    </p>

    <h2>3 · Four lanes, never blended</h2>
    <p>
      For anything with a return number, the site identifies which of four quantities it is: <em>historical realised</em>
      (what happened, to whom, over what period), <em>contractual</em> (what a binding agreement pays, and the credit
      standing of the payer), <em>model-estimated</em> (what arithmetic derives from inspectable assumptions), or
      <em>speculative upside</em> (possible, unsupported, labelled). The <a href={href('/research/high-return')}>high-return
      research</a> applies this to every avenue where the distinction matters most.
    </p>

    <h2>4 · No fabricated data — ever</h2>
    <p>
      Where a number is unavailable, the page says so. Where an example is constructed to show a mechanism, it is
      labelled illustrative and uses round, obviously synthetic figures. No market level, fund return, rate or statistic
      appears here that cannot be traced to a registry source.
    </p>

    <h2>5 · Calculators are code, and code is tested</h2>
    <p>
      The numerical library behind the {`calculators`} — SIP and XIRR solvers, bond pricing, duration and convexity,
      tax engine, Monte Carlo with a fixed seed — is covered by a test suite that runs on every build. Tests include
      hand-computed known values (a bond must price at par when coupon equals yield; the SIP formula against a
      hand-worked projection) and invariants across all calculators. Functions return “not available” when a quantity
      is undefined, so a fabricated zero can never appear in an output.
    </p>

    <h2>6 · AI assistance, disclosed</h2>
    <p>
      Drafting is performed with AI assistance under human direction: source retrieval, structure and first drafts are
      machine-produced, then reduced to what the sources actually support. Verification claims are limited to what is
      true — figures are checked against the cited documents, and the review status of every tax rule is published.
      Where review has not happened, the page says so rather than implying it has.
    </p>

    <h2>7 · Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Errors are corrected on the page, logged on the <a href={href('/about/corrections')}>corrections page</a>, and
      the article's updated date changes. A correction is treated as an improvement to the archive, not an embarrassment;
      the alternative — quietly editing — is what destroys trust in financial writing.
    </p>

    <h2>8 · What the method cannot do</h2>
    <p>
      It cannot know your circumstances, cannot evaluate an unlisted product you are being shown, and cannot replace
      the primary documents it cites. It is a map with the legend printed on it; the territory is the official sources.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
